\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The sign edge as one column of the chain}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{On the sign edge the invariant functions have rank #{2}, by
the quotient in \ref{fdip-0123}. The one-edge matrix has
characteristic polynomial
#{t^2-10t+12}, and its larger eigenvalue is
#{\frac{10+\sqrt{52}}{2}}. An
environment constant scales the sector sum and leaves this
matrix in place. A fermion that is a single sign line has
[Yukawa map](fdip-011M) equal to #{0}. Exchanging a trivial line with a sign line
produces the onsite values #{m+y} and #{m-y}.}

\proof{
\p{The group of \ref{fdip-012H} is #{\{1,-1\}}, and the
stabilizer is trivial. \ref{fdip-0123} identifies the invariant
functions with the functions on the residual quotient of the
set of links. The residual group is trivial, so for one edge
that quotient is the group itself and the rank is #{2}. The
same example gives the matrix and the polynomial
#{t^2-10t+12}. \ref{fdip-012X} proves that
#{\frac{10+\sqrt{52}}{2}} is the larger eigenvalue and that the
Rayleigh inequality holds. \ref{fdip-0135} records the
determinant #{12}.}

\p{\ref{fdip-013A} multiplies the sector sum by the environment
constant and leaves the matrix unchanged, so the polynomial
remains #{t^2-10t+12}. \ref{fdip-011O} shows that an
equivariant Yukawa map on a single sign line vanishes, and that
the exchange of a trivial line with a sign line has onsite
eigenvalues #{m+y} and #{m-y}.}
}
